stage: 3
finding: Medium — a runner signal does not cancel the selected integration loop. `bun /tmp/shallot-review-signal-probe.ts` interrupted the first of two rows and returned `exitCode:1`, but `laterRowStarted:true`; summary was `1 passed, 1 failed` and the report contained a passing second row. Thus SIGTERM can trigger new work after interruption. Medium — the `run()` fallback can preserve a false-green summary and invalid JUnit instead of using `failedJunit`: `bun /tmp/shallot-review-junit-probe.ts` returned exit 1 but printed `shallot test: 2 passed, 0 failed`; report was only `<?xml version="1.0"?><testsuites tests="2"><testsuite tests="2">`, and Python `xml.etree.ElementTree.parse` failed with `ParseError: no element found`. `readJunitSummary` accepts partial XML by regex, while `appendRunnerFailure` increments only attributes present and does not ensure an appended testcase or valid document. Medium — leak detection is not portable across Linux `ps` implementations: `docker run --rm --entrypoint sh discord-mcp:local -lc 'ps -axo pid=,pgid=,command='` returned BusyBox 1.37 `ps: unrecognized option: x`. On a surviving group `groupProcesses()` throws, and `teardownGroup()` propagates that before grace/KILL completion; no report path handles this failure. Debian/procps and macOS accept the command.
